Here's a compact visual report on why an OpenCloset eval harness matters â three key bullets plus a scorecard.

<transient-window id="b7e4a1" title="OpenCloset Eval Harness â Why It Matters" summary="Three-bullet summary and compact scorecard for the OpenCloset eval harness.">
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<style>
  :root {
    --bg: #0f1117;
    --card: #1a1d27;
    --border: #2a2d3a;
    --accent: #6ee7b7;
    --accent2: #7dd3fc;
    --text: #e2e4ea;
    --muted: #9ca3af;
    --gold: #fbbf24;
    --red: #f87171;
    --green: #4ade80;
  }
  * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
  body {
    font-family: 'Segoe UI', system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--text);
    padding: 24px;
    min-height: 100vh;
  }
  .header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 24px;
  }
  .header-icon {
    width: 36px; height: 36px;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--accent), var(--accent2));
    border-radius: 8px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    font-size: 18px;
  }
  .header h1 {
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .header p {
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 2px;
  }

  .bullets {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }
  .bullet {
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 14px 16px;
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    align-items: flex-start;
  }
  .bullet-num {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 26px; height: 26px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--bg);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 13px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  }
  .bullet h3 {
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 3px;
  }
  .bullet p {
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  .scorecard {
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 16px;
  }
  .scorecard h2 {
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }
  .score-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .score-row:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .score-label {
    width: 130px;
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .score-bar-bg {
    flex: 1;
    height: 8px;
    background: var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .score-bar {
    height: 100%;
    border-radius: 4px;
    transition: width 0.6s ease;
  }
  .score-val {
    width: 36px;
    text-align: right;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .bar-green  { background: var(--green); }
  .bar-gold   { background: var(--gold); }
  .bar-red    { background: var(--red); }
  .val-green  { color: var(--green); }
  .val-gold   { color: var(--gold); }
  .val-red    { color: var(--red); }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="header">
  <div class="header-icon">ð</div>
  <div>
    <h1>OpenCloset Eval Harness â Why It Matters</h1>
    <p>Local-first agent evaluation for wardrobe &amp; style workflows</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="bullets">
  <div class="bullet">
    <div class="bullet-num">1</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Ground-truth validation for agent decisions</h3>
      <p>Without an eval harness, style recommendations and outfit completions are unmeasured guesses. The harness provides deterministic test cases â known inputs, known expected outputs â so every agent change is regression-safe.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="bullet">
    <div class="bullet-num">2</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Reproducible benchmarking across models</h3>
      <p>Compare local LLMs (llama.cpp, Ollama) and cloud APIs on the same wardrobe tasks. Score accuracy, latency, and token cost side-by-side so you pick the right model for your hardware.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="bullet">
    <div class="bullet-num">3</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Structured feedback loop for prompt engineering</h3>
      <p>Each eval run produces a scorecard. When a prompt tweak drops "outfit coherence" from 0.82 to 0.71, you catch it immediately â no waiting for user complaints.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="scorecard">
  <h2>Scorecard</h2>

  <div class="score-row">
    <span class="score-label">Outfit Coherence</span>
    <div class="score-bar-bg"><div class="score-bar bar-green" style="width:82%"></div></div>
    <span class="score-val val-green">0.82</span>
  </div>
  <div class="score-row">
    <span class="score-label">Style Consistency</span>
    <div class="score-bar-bg"><div class="score-bar bar-green" style="width:76%"></div></div>
    <span class="score-val val-green">0.76</span>
  </div>
  <div class="score-row">
    <span class="score-label">Context Recall</span>
    <div class="score-bar-bg"><div class="score-bar bar-gold" style="width:64%"></div></div>
    <span class="score-val val-gold">0.64</span>
  </div>
  <div class="score-row">
    <span class="score-label">Hallucination Rate</span>
    <div class="score-bar-bg"><div class="score-bar bar-red" style="width:18%"></div></div>
    <span class="score-val val-red">0.18</span>
  </div>
  <div class="score-row">
    <span class="score-label">Token Efficiency</span>
    <div class="score-bar-bg"><div class="score-bar bar-gold" style="width:58%"></div></div>
    <span class="score-val val-gold">0.58</span>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
window.__captureState = () => ({});
window.__restoreState = (s) => {};
</script>
</body>
</html>
</transient-window>

The three bullets cover the core reasons: **ground-truth validation**, **cross-model benchmarking**, and a **structured feedback loop** for prompt engineering. The scorecard shows five dimensions â outfit coherence, style consistency, context recall, hallucination rate, and token efficiency â with color-coded bars so you can spot weak spots at a glance.
